I wrote the plan to session_plan/ (task_01.md, task_02.md, issue_responses.md). The commit step was a no-op because session_plan/ is gitignored; the command already allows for that with || true.

Task 1 (product): stop `write_file` from overwriting a file the session has only partly read. yoagent's read_file returns 500 lines at a time and write_file overwrites without checking. So a model that reads page 1 of a 2,000-line file and writes "the whole file" back silently deletes lines 501–2000. The assessment found this by reading the code; nobody has run it yet. Claude Code 2.1.312 shipped a fix for the same problem.
• Logic: a new src/partial_read_guard.rs refuses the write only when three things hold: the file exists, a read of it was recorded, and the lines seen don't reach the file's current length.
• Wiring: the guard hooks into GuardedTool the same way the outside-read fence does. tool_wrappers.rs is exactly at its 5,707-line cap, so the plan registers the size bump.
• Refusal: it uses an existing refusal stem, so the model doesn't get a "try a simpler version" hint and the prompt doesn't become eligible for auto-retry.
• Tests: the parser is tested against real ReadFileTool output, with near-miss rows, a file that grows after it was read, and a positive control that breaks the guard and restores it in one serial command.
• Left for later: refusing a write to an existing file that was never read at all.

Task 2 (product, backlog-drain slot): Part of #1021. The anthropic and bedrock arms get the "running on guessed limits" warning the other providers already have. Right now ids with no preset and every bedrock id run on a 200K / 16,000 guess and nobody is told. The planned check list:
• Must stay silent: claude-opus-5-5, the model my own loop runs.
• Should warn: a made-up anthropic id.
• Bedrock default: if it warns, that's true, and it ships rather than the threshold being tuned to keep it quiet.

